"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkgrc||=[]).push([[47597],{7910(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"citeKey":"ye2026pkas","slug":"ye-2026-pkas-predictive-kvcache-aware-scheduling-for-81cf","title":"PKAS: Predictive KVCache-Aware Scheduling for Faster LLM and Transformer Inferences","authors":["J. Ye","A. Maurya","K. Chitty-Venkata","B. Nicolae","A. Kougkas","X.-H. Sun"],"venue":"35th International Symposium on High-Performance Parallel and Distributed Computing (HPDC 2026)","type":"Conference","entryType":"inproceedings","date":"July, 2026","year":2026,"era":"iit","month":7,"doi":"10.1145/3806645.3807819","abstract":"With rising popularity of LLMs, the performance, scalability, and resource-efficiency of inferences become a crucial challenge. The core part of the inference process is the KV cache, which avoids recomputing intermediate attention states, and the batching strategy that batches multiple requests per forward pass to leverage GPU parallelism. KV cache memory grows linearly with sequence length and batch sizes, easily exceeding the limited GPU memory capacity. State-of-the-art inference runtimes use continuous batching to maximize GPU utilization by interleaving the processing of new requests (i.e., prefill requests) with ongoing generation requests (i.e., decode requests). However, existing schedulers greedily admit prefill requests without considering the future KV cache memory required to successfully run the decode phases. This shortsighted approach causes frequent KV cache overflows, which in turn trigger preemption and recomputation of requests, severely degrading both throughput and latency. We propose PKAS, a Predictive KV Cache-Aware Scheduling algorithm to mitigate this inefficiency by reducing preemptions. PKAS uses a low-overhead technique to simulate future KV cache utilization and guide the admissibility for new request candidates. Combined with lightweight output-length predictions, PKAS can make better batching decisions, preventing KV cache overflows and drastically reducing preemptions. Evaluations on diverse models and workloads show that PKAS achieves up to 7.34x higher throughput and 8x lower latency compared to state-of-the-art scheduling, with the largest gains on long-context workloads where KV cache pressure is high.","hasAbstract":true,"lede":"We propose PKAS, a Predictive KV Cache-Aware Scheduling algorithm to mitigate this inefficiency by reducing preemptions.","pages":"402\u2013414","links":{"pdf":"https://grc.iit.edu/papers/2026/ye-2026-pkas-predictive-kvcache-aware-scheduling-for-81cf.pdf","bibtex":"https://grc.iit.edu/papers/2026/ye-2026-pkas-predictive-kvcache-aware-scheduling-for-81cf-bibtex.bib","citation":"https://grc.iit.edu/papers/2026/ye-2026-pkas-predictive-kvcache-aware-scheduling-for-81cf-citation.txt"},"tags":["KV Cache Management","LLM Inference Serving","Scheduling"],"_projectIds":["deepio","labios","iowarp","chronolog","mpi4ai"],"grcSelected":false,"status":"published","visibility":"public","projectIds":["iowarp","labios","deepio","chronolog","mpi4ai"],"memberAuthors":["ye-jie","nicolae-bogdan","kougkas-anthony","sun-xian-he"],"funding":[{"agency":"NSF","award":"OAC-2313154","projectId":"labios"},{"agency":"NSF","award":"OAC-2411318","projectId":"iowarp"},{"agency":"NSF","award":"OAC-2104013","projectId":"chronolog"},{"agency":"NSF","award":"CSSI-2411386"},{"agency":"NSF","award":"OAC-2514056","projectId":"mpi4ai"},{"agency":"ANL","award":"DE-AC02-06CH11357"}]}')}}]);